@meta
id: 04-14
title: When counting is the wrong answer
chapter: 04
type: lesson
skills: count-prefix, relativenumber, hjkl
par: 36
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl-G, type a line number
open: seed.sql
vimrc: set relativenumber number

@brief
A hundred and twelve lines of seed data and two small jobs. One is
close, one is not, and they want different tools.

  1. Line 8 has lost its trailing semicolon. Put it back.

  2. Line 104 is the QA test account. Mark it by appending
     `  -- qa fixture` — two spaces, then the comment — to the end of
     that line.

There is no arrow-key route to line 104 that you would be willing to
watch, so do not look for one.

The first is a counted jump: the margin tells you the distance, you type
it. The second is not, and the reason is on your screen — line 104 is not
on it. A relative number only exists for a line you can see, so there is
nothing to read and nothing to count.

For that one, give the command line the line number instead: `:104` and
Enter puts the cursor on line 104. It is the same command line you have
been using for `:w` and `:q`.

@keys
| `7j` | down seven lines |
| `:104` `Enter` | go to line 104 |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `k` | up one line |

@hints
Do the near one with a count read off the margin, exactly as before.
--
Do not count your way to line 104. Ask for it by number on the command
line.
--
`:104` `Enter` lands you on that line whatever is on screen. From there
it is the same `A` and the same typing as the first fix.

@solution
    7j A ; <Esc>
    :104<Enter> A <space><space>-- qa fixture <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

The rule of thumb, and it is worth writing down: **count when you can see
it, name it when you cannot.**

Counts are exact and cheap for short hops, because the number is already
printed next to the line you are looking at. The moment the target is off
screen, working out the number costs more than the jump saves, and an
approximate jump followed by hunting around is worse than either.

The tools for naming a place instead of counting to it arrive shortly.
Chapter 7 gives you `104G`, which is what people actually type for a line
number, and `gg` and `G` for the ends of the file. Chapter 12 gives you
`/CUS-402` and Enter, which is how you would really have found this one —
you knew what the row looked like, not where it lived.

Until then: if the count is bigger than about twenty, you are probably
using the wrong tool, and after chapter 12 you will be using the wrong
tool at any size. Counts stay excellent for the short hops that make up
most of your day.

